# /etc/synguard/rules.d/50-default-deny.rules
# SynapseOS synguard — the rules that ACT, shipped armed
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS FILE IS LIVE. Unlike 40-enforce.rules.example beside it, this one ends in
# `.rules`, so synguard loads it and these verdicts are real.
#
# Until this existed, SynapseOS shipped 55 rules and not one of them carried
# `deny` or `quarantine`. The daemon ran with --mode enforce, so acting was
# permitted; nothing loaded ever asked for it. synguard detected and alerted and
# never once stopped anything — which is a defensible default and a strange
# thing to leave permanently, because a security monitor that has never acted
# has never had its acting path exercised either.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT IS IN HERE, AND WHY IT IS SO SHORT
#
# A false positive costs a process TREE. sg_kill_tree() takes the matched
# process and its descendants, and sg_is_protected() refuses only PID 0/1,
# kernel threads, synguard, synapd, synnet and synsh. Your desktop, your editor
# and your browser are not protected. Quarantine is gentler — SIGSTOP, and
# `kill -CONT` puts it back — but a frozen process tree is still somebody's
# afternoon.
#
# So the bar for a rule in this file is not "is this suspicious". It is:
#
#   1. does anything on a working SynapseOS desktop do this, ever? Not
#      "rarely" — ever. Every rule below is something for which the honest
#      answer is no.
#   2. if it fires wrongly anyway, is the cost recoverable?
#   3. is the damage it stops ONGOING rather than instantaneous? The userspace
#      path kills after the syscall has already succeeded, so a rule whose
#      whole value was in preventing the first act buys nothing there.
#
# Three rules clear that bar. The ones that do not are listed at the bottom
# with the reason, because "we considered it and here is what it breaks" is
# worth more to whoever reads this next than a shorter file.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠ THESE ARE AFTER-THE-FACT KILLS UNLESS YOU ARM THE KERNEL PATH
#
# synapse_kmod observes with kprobes; it never returns -EPERM. A deny rule on
# the userspace path means "it does not get to continue", not "it does not
# happen". Two of the three rules below LOWER to the BPF LSM, where the syscall
# itself is refused — but the gate is shut unless synguard runs with
# --bpf-enforce, which the shipped unit does not pass.
#
# To arm it:
#
#     sudo systemctl edit synguard
#     [Service]
#     ExecStart=
#     ExecStart=/usr/bin/synguard --foreground --mode enforce \
#         --rules /etc/synguard/rules.d/ --bpf-enforce
#
# The way out if that ever locks you out: add `synapse.bpf_enforce=0` to the
# kernel command line at the boot menu, and synguard comes up detect-only. The
# gate also fails open if synguard dies or wedges, and refuses to act for the
# first 30 seconds after attaching, so you get a login even when a rule is
# badly wrong.
#
#     journalctl -u synguard | grep bpf-lsm     — what is actually armed
#
# ⚠ THE PRIORITIES HERE ARE 0 ON PURPOSE, and it is a real trade rather than a
# tidy number. 00-base.rules opens with allow-synguard and allow-synapd at
# priority 1, both `comm <name>` with no path, so they match any event on any
# file. Nothing can be proved disjoint from them, and a deny rule BELOW them
# cannot lower to the kernel at all. Above them it can — and it then applies to
# synguard and synapd too. That is fine for a file neither daemon opens, which
# is exactly what these two protect, and it would be wrong for anything they
# touch. Pick per rule; do not copy the 0 without asking that question.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── /etc/ld.so.preload ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The classic userland-rootkit hook: every dynamically linked process started
# afterwards loads whatever is listed there. No Arch package manages this file
# and on a clean system it does not exist at all, so there is no legitimate
# opener to false-positive on — which is what puts it over the bar.
#
# ⚠ `access write`, AND THIS COST A MACHINE TO LEARN.
#
# It shipped without it, on the reasoning that refusing the READ is what
# neuters an already-planted preload: ld.so opens this file on every exec, and
# glibc treats a failed open as "no preload", so the rule would degrade an
# infected machine to a working one. That is true — and only on the KERNEL
# path, which can refuse an open. The userspace path cannot refuse anything.
# It can only kill, after the fact, and what it kills is the opener's whole
# process TREE.
#
# So on a machine where /etc/ld.so.preload existed and the BPF gate was not
# armed — which is every machine, since --bpf-enforce is not in the shipped
# unit — EVERY EXEC ON THE SYSTEM matched this rule and had its tree torn
# down. Including the `rm` you would use to delete the file. Including sshd.
# Observed 2026-08-20: a test that planted the file turned a working laptop
# into one where no command could complete.
#
# A read is what a working system does with this file. A write is what an
# attack does. `access write` is the only version of this rule that is safe on
# both paths, and the neutering property has to go with it: it was only ever
# available to the kernel path, and it is not worth a rule that bricks a box on
# the other one.
#
# On a clean system the file is absent, lookup fails at ENOENT, and nothing
# fires either way — this rule costs nothing until the day it matters.
#
# Lowers to the kernel: an exact path, no wildcard, and priority 0 puts it
# above the allows. /etc/ld.so.preload is on the kmod's sensitive_paths list,
# so the userspace path sees it too when the gate is shut.
rule deny-ld-preload {
    event    open
    path     /etc/ld.so.preload
    access   write
    verdict  deny
    priority 0
}


# ── The positive control ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A rule that never fires is indistinguishable from a rule that is not working,
# and every other rule in this file is one you hope never fires. This is the
# one you can trip on demand, so "is enforcement actually on" has an answer
# that does not require being attacked.
#
# ⚠ The file must EXIST or this is unenforceable on BOTH paths while looking
# perfectly armed: lsm/file_open only fires for an open that reaches a file,
# and the kprobe path sees the ENOENT attempt but can only kill afterwards, by
# which time a short-lived opener has exited. synguard.service recreates it in
# ExecStartPre for exactly this reason — if you move this rule, move that too.
#
# Verify, as root:
#
#     systemd-run -q --collect cat /var/lib/synguard/bpf-canary
#
# Expect "Operation not permitted" — root, refused on a file root owns, which
# only an LSM can do — and `bpf-lsm: denied=` incrementing in the journal.
# ⚠ Under systemd-run, NOT in your shell: a deny tears down the opener's
# process tree and your shell is in it.
rule deny-bpf-canary {
    event    open
    path     /var/lib/synguard/bpf-canary
    verdict  deny
    priority 0
}


# ── Execution out of the top of /dev ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# /dev is a devtmpfs of device nodes. A regular executable file sitting there
# and being run is not something a working system produces; it is a well-worn
# way to stage a payload somewhere nobody audits. 00-base.rules already
# escalates this to the AI classifier at priority 35; this acts on it first.
#
# QUARANTINE, not deny, and the reason is the whole point of the verdict: if
# this ever fires you want to look at the process rather than lose it. SIGSTOP
# on the tree, `kill -CONT <pid>` to release it — recoverable, which rule 2 of
# the bar above asks for. It also stays on the userspace path by definition; an
# LSM hook can return -EPERM and nothing else, so a quarantine rule is never
# silently demoted into a refusal that destroys the evidence you chose
# quarantine to keep.
#
# ⚠ ONE LEVEL, deliberately. `path` is FNM_PATHNAME, so `*` stops at a `/`:
# this covers /dev/foo and NOT /dev/shm/foo. The /dev/*/* rule that would catch
# the second is NOT armed here — see below.
#
# Below the allows, because unlike the two above this one has no need to be
# exempt from nothing, and priority 30 keeps it ahead of 00-base's escalate.
rule quarantine-exec-from-dev {
    event    exec
    path     /dev/*
    verdict  quarantine
    priority 30
}


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY NOT ARMED
#
# All of these are in 40-enforce.rules.example with their own notes. They are
# repeated here with what specifically breaks on SynapseOS, so nobody has to
# rediscover it by arming one.
#
#   exec from /dev/*/*  — catches /dev/shm, which is a real staging ground and
#       also a real tmpfs that legitimate software runs things out of. The
#       one-level rule above is the part with no false positives; this is the
#       part with some. Arm it if you know your workload.
#
#   deny-unexpected-module-load — SynapseOS ships synapse_kmod as a DKMS
#       module and this desktop runs the NVIDIA driver. Every DKMS rebuild and
#       every driver update loads modules from programs not on 00-base's
#       allow-list. This rule would fire on a routine `pacman -Syu`, kill the
#       tree it fired in, and leave a half-installed kernel module. It is the
#       single most tempting rule in the example file and the one most certain
#       to break this distribution.
#
#   deny-shell-from-sshd — the reverse-shell signature, and the highest-value
#       rule in the example. It is not armed because whether it is safe depends
#       on a thing that varies per user: sshd execs the account's LOGIN SHELL,
#       and `ssh host <command>` runs `$SHELL -c`. The example's `path
#       /usr/bin/sh` misses bash and misses synsh — so on most accounts it is a
#       rule that can never fire, and on an account whose shell IS /usr/bin/sh
#       it locks that account out of SSH entirely. A rule that is either inert
#       or a lockout, depending on a setting this file cannot see, does not
#       belong in a shipped default. Tailor it to the services you run and
#       watch it in `alert` first.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ IF A RULE HERE EVER LOCKS YOU OUT — READ THIS BEFORE YOU REINSTALL
#
# It has happened once already, so this is a procedure rather than a
# precaution. A deny rule on the USERSPACE path kills the opener's process
# tree, and if the thing it matches is something every program does, every
# program on the machine is torn down — including the one you would use to fix
# it. That is what `deny-ld-preload` did before it carried `access write`: ld.so
# opens that file on every exec.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED TO REINSTALL. Nothing is damaged — no filesystem, no package
# state, no configuration. One rule is matching something it should not, and
# the machine is fine the moment synguard is not running.
#
#   1. At the boot menu press `e` (GRUB) or `e`/Tab (limine) and append to the
#      kernel line:
#
#          systemd.mask=synguard.service
#
#      That masks it for THAT BOOT ONLY; nothing persists. Boot, fix the rule
#      or delete the file it is matching, reboot.
#
#   2. If that will not take, `init=/bin/bash` on the same line skips systemd
#      altogether — no synguard at all. Then:
#
#          mount -o remount,rw /
#          rm -f /etc/synguard/rules.d/50-default-deny.rules
#
#   3. `synapse.bpf_enforce=0` is NOT this. It disarms the KERNEL gate only,
#      and a userspace deny keeps killing regardless. It is the escape hatch
#      for a bad kernel refusal, not for a bad rule.
#
# ⚠ AND IF THE MACHINE IS ALREADY IN THAT STATE AND STILL RESPONDING:
# sg_kill_tree SIGSTOPs a tree before tearing it down, and a teardown that does
# not complete leaves the process STOPPED rather than dead. So things are
# usually resumable — `kill -CONT %1` will let a stopped command finish. And
# `[[ -e /some/path ]]` is a shell BUILTIN, so it can check state without
# needing an exec that would be stopped in its turn.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TURNING THIS OFF
#
# Nothing here needs to be edited to disable it — the file is the unit of
# choice:
#
#     sudo mv /etc/synguard/rules.d/50-default-deny.rules{,.off}
#     sudo systemctl reload synguard
#
# `.off` is not `.rules`, so synguard stops loading it. `journalctl -u synguard`
# reports the rule count at every load, which is how you confirm it took.
